50. DATA PRIVACY
Telemetry shall follow the same security principles as the application.
The observability subsystem must not become an uncontrolled copy of project data.
Telemetry shall minimize:
personal information
project content
customer secrets
authentication credentials
AI prompts containing sensitive information
file contents
Only information required for operational diagnosis should be retained.

51. SECRET REDACTION
The telemetry pipeline shall automatically prevent collection of:
passwords
access tokens
refresh tokens
API keys
private keys
session secrets
database credentials
Sensitive fields shall be redacted before telemetry leaves the application boundary.

52. RETENTION
Operational telemetry shall have defined retention periods.
The retention policy shall distinguish:
high-volume metrics
application logs
security logs
traces
critical incident records
Operational telemetry retention shall not override the separate project-data retention and provenance policy established in later Stack 36.0.

53. TELEMETRY STORAGE
The observability storage system shall be independent of the primary project database where practical.
The architecture becomes:
POSTGRESQL
    ↓
PROJECT SYSTEM OF RECORD

OBSERVABILITY STORAGE
    ↓
OPERATIONAL SYSTEM OF RECORD
Neither system should be required to store the other's entire data model.

54. FAILURE ISOLATION
A failure in telemetry collection must not normally cause the OneDraft application itself to fail.
Telemetry shall therefore use:
buffering
batching
timeouts
backpressure
local fallback
where appropriate.
The application remains operational if the telemetry backend becomes temporarily unavailable.

55. TELEMETRY BACKPRESSURE
Telemetry systems shall protect application workloads from excessive telemetry volume.
The system may:
sample traces
aggregate metrics
batch logs
drop low-priority diagnostic events
Critical operational and security events shall not be discarded merely because telemetry volume is high.

56. TRACE SAMPLING
Trace sampling may be used at scale.
Sampling policies may distinguish:
successful routine operations
slow operations
failed operations
security events
critical jobs
Failures and anomalous operations should receive higher tracing priority.

57. DIAGNOSTIC SNAPSHOTS
For significant failures, the system should be capable of producing a diagnostic snapshot containing:
